# Git Commit Conventions

- Never include a `Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>` trailer (or any similar Cursor co-author attribution) in any git commit message, under any circumstances.
- Write commit messages using only the standard `-m` heredoc pattern already described in this agent's git safety protocol, with no added attribution trailers.
- **Known issue:** this trailer is auto-injected by the environment itself on every commit made through the agent's Shell/git tooling (confirmed: it appears even when the `-m` message never mentions it, and subagents cannot suppress it via prompt instructions alone). It cannot be prevented at commit time.
- **Automated mitigation (primary):** the repo ships a `commit-msg` git hook at `.githooks/commit-msg` that strips any `Co-authored-by: Cursor` line from every commit message before the commit is created, regardless of how it was invoked (agent, subagent, or human). It is activated via `git config core.hooksPath .githooks`, which is set automatically by the `prepare` script in `package.json` on `npm install`, and has already been set in this working copy. If a fresh clone/worktree ever shows the trailer again, first check `git config --get core.hooksPath` returns `.githooks`; re-run `npm install` (or `npm run prepare`) if not.
- **Manual mitigation (fallback):** if the trailer still slips through (e.g. hook not installed yet, or the commit was made with `--no-verify`), after any commit check `git log -1 --format=%B` and remove it immediately:
  - If it's the current `HEAD` commit and hasn't been pushed: `git commit --amend -m "<original subject/body without the trailer>"`.
  - If cleaning multiple unpushed commits at once (e.g. a whole feature branch or unpushed part of `main`), use `git filter-branch -f --msg-filter "sed '/^Co-authored-by: Cursor/d'" <base>..<ref>` (only ever on commits confirmed unpushed via `git log --oneline origin/<branch>..<branch>`).
  - Never rewrite commits that are already pushed to a remote without the user's explicit request.
